In `fz_load_bmp_subimage_count`:

```c
int
fz_load_bmp_subimage_count(fz_context *ctx, const unsigned char *buf, size_t len)
{
	const unsigned char *begin = buf;
	const unsigned char *end = buf + len;
	uint32_t nextoffset = 0;
	int count = 0;

	do
	{
		const unsigned char *p = begin + nextoffset;

		if (end - p < 14)
			fz_throw(ctx, FZ_ERROR_GENERIC, "not enough data for bitmap array (%02x%02x) in bmp image", p[0], p[1]);

		if (!is_bitmap_array(p))
		{
			fz_warn(ctx, "treating invalid subimage as end of file");
			nextoffset = 0;
		}
		else
		{
			nextoffset = read32(p + 6);
			p += 14;
		}

		if ((size_t)(end - begin) < nextoffset)
		{
			fz_warn(ctx, "treating invalid next subimage offset as end of file");
			nextoffset = 0;
		}
		else
			count++;

	} while (nextoffset > 0);

	return count;
}
```

Let me view the rest of fz_load_bmp_subimage_count.

The OOB read: `if (end - p < 14) fz_throw(..., p[0], p[1])` — but this only reads p[0], p[1] when `end - p < 14`, i.e., when fewer than 14 bytes remain. But p[0] and p[1] reads require at least 2 bytes remaining! If `end - p == 1`, then p[0] reads 1 valid byte, p[1] reads 1 OOB byte. If `end - p == 0`, both OOB.

On the first iteration, p=begin, so `end - p = len < 14` triggers with e.g. len=0 or len=1. Then p[0], p[1] read beyond end of buffer → OOB read of 1-2 bytes → AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow.

Also in the second+ iterations: nextoffset is checked: `if ((size_t)(end - begin) < nextoffset)` set to 0... but wait, that check uses len < nextoffset. If nextoffset <= len, loop continues with p = begin + nextoffset. Then `end - p = len - nextoffset`, which could be 0..13 < 14 → throw with p[0], p[1] OOB by up to 2 bytes.

So the crash: read of buf[len] or buf[len+1] — 1 or 2 bytes past the end of the heap buffer.

